  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 345 AM CDT Wed Jun 10 2026\n\nHeat Wave continues over Central/Southeast IL Today and Thu...\nHigh temperatures today and Thursday will be around 90F to \nlower 90s. This heat combined with muggy dew points into the low\nto mid 70s to give afternoon heat indices in the upper 90s and \nlower 100s (100-105F) today and Thursday. HREF has a 20-40% \nchance of heat indices rising over 100F in southeast IL this \nafternoon HREF has a 30-60% chance of heat indices over 100F \nfrom Beardstown to Springfield to Terre Haute south Thu \nafternoon. This heat wave may lead to enhanced heat risk for \nvulnerable populations and we will be approaching heat advisory\ncriteria of 105F in out southern counties this afternoon and Thu\nafternoon.\n\nSevere Storms Possible This Afternoon through Thu Evening...\nUnsettled weather pattern to continue over IL through Thu night\nwith daily chances of showers and thunderstorms. IL to remain \nin a moist/unstable tropical air mass through Thu evening \nproviding fuel for strong to severe storms and heavy rains. The\nlatest CAMs like HRRR continue to show a band/wave of convection\nmoving into the IL river valley during mid/late afternoon, and\nanother wave/line of convection pushing further se into central\nIL during this evening and early overnight before weakening into\nsoutheast IL during overnight. Moderate instability develops \ntoday with MLCAPES of 2-3k j/kg though modest Bulk Shear values \nof 20-25 kts (typical of mid summer). SPC Day1 outlook has a \nslight risk of severe storms this afternoon/evening from \nDanville to Taylorville nw and enhanced risk from Canton to \nPeoria to Minonk nw. Damaging winds are biggest threat with \n25-30% risk over nw CWA including Level 1 for greater than 75 \nmph winds. Areas from I-55 nw have 2-5% risk of tornadoes and \n15-29% risk of large hail. There is also risk of larger than 2 \ninch hail from Rushville to Galesburg nw. WI, eastern Iowa and \nnorthern/nw IL are in the highest risk of strongest storms. WPC \nday1 ERO has slight risk of excessive rainfall from I-72 north \nand marginal risk south of I-72. PW values rise to 1.6-2 inches\ntoday. LPMM shows a few swaths of 2-3 inches of rain (locally\nhigher) north of I-70 this afternoon and tonight.\n\nAfter a brief lull in convection late tonight into Thu morning,\nwe will likely see a developing line of strong to severe\nthunderstorms moving se into the IL river valley during mid to\nlate Thu afternoon, and over rest of central IL Thu evening,\ngetting into southeast IL late evening/early overnight Thu night.\nVery unstable air mass ahead of storms with MLCAPES 3-4k j/kg\nand 0-6km Bulk Shear values 25-35 kts and lowest 1 km shear of\n15-25 kts. SPC Day2 Outlook has Enhanced Risk over central IL\nfrom mid Thu afternoon into Thu evening, with slight risk in\nsoutheast IL. Damaging winds is greatest threat with 30-45% risk\nin central IL (highest in Knox, Stark and Marshall counties).\nThere is also a level 1 for winds over 75 mph possible in the\nenhanced risk area north of a Paris to Shelbyville line. There\nis a level 1 for hail over 2 inches in diameter nw half of CWA\nfrom Rantoul to Litchfield nw. There is a 5-10% risk of\ntornadoes in the enhanced risk area, including level 1 for up to\nEF2 tornadoes from Pittsfield to Rantoul nw. WPC Day 2 ERO has \nslight risk of excessive rainfall Thu afternoon/Thu night north \nof I-72 with 25% risk of rainfall exceeding Flash Flood Guidance\nnw of the IL river. \n\nCold front to pass southeast of IL during overnight Thu night, \nwith quieter, cooler and less humid weather on tap for Friday \nand Friday night. We have slight chances of showers and \nthunderstorms by Saturday afternoon over western/sw CWA but most\nof CWA should continue with dry weather through at least sunset\nSaturday. Breezy SSW winds to bring a bit warmer and more humid\nair back into central/se IL Saturday, with highs in the mid 80s\nand dewpoints in the 60s. A broad mid/upper level trof over the\nMidwest and Great Lakes region this weekend into early next \nweek to keep temps and humidity levels in check. Some weak short\nwaves within the upper level flow to bring periodic chances of \nshowers and thunderstorms to the area, starting Sat night and \nSunday and going into the middle of next week. The risk of \nsevere storms appears low overall for IL from Friday through \nearly next week per SPC Day 3 and 4-8 outlooks along with CIPS \nand CSU-MLP outlooks. \n\nThe Climate Prediction Center (CPC) 8-14 Day Outlook for June\n17-23rd has a 40-50% chance of above normal precipitation over\ncentral and southeast IL, with temperatures trending near\nnormal.",
